et them","p_rec":"If New York (JFK)'s climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, New York (JFK) has set <strong>71 standing record lows</strong>, fewer than the 125 that chance alone would give, and <strong>160 record highs</strong>, more than the 125 that chance alone would give. The 2010s hold the most record highs of any decade (66). New York (JFK)'s all-time high is 104\u00b0F, on July 3, 1966.","t_chg":"New York (JFK) has warmed, days and nights alike","p_chg":"Across 77 complete years: New York (JFK)'s average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.29\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.17 to +0.41), about +2.2\u00b0F since 1949. New York (JFK)'s average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.37\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.26 to +0.49), about +2.8\u00b0F since 1949.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 9 of 12 months. Highs rose in 8 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"New York (JFK)'s yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.70 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 43.2 inches a year. The wettest year was 1983 (59.12 in); the driest, 1965 (25.38 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 5 daily record highs, 4 daily record lows"}}